# See https://help.github.com/articles/ignoring-files/ for more about ignoring files.

# dependencies
/node_modules
/.pnp
.pnp.*
.yarn/*
!.yarn/patches
!.yarn/plugins
!.yarn/releases
!.yarn/versions

# testing
/coverage

# next.js
/.next/
/out/

# production
/build

# misc
.DS_Store
*.pem

# debug
npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
.pnpm-debug.log*

# env files — real secrets ignored, but COMMIT the template (.env.example has no secrets)
.env*
!.env.example

# vercel
.vercel

# editor settings (local watcher excludes — keep the 18GB Mac from freezing on dual-IDE indexing)
.vscode/

# typescript
*.tsbuildinfo
next-env.d.ts

# ── prep / strategy / personal — LOCAL ONLY, never publish to the public event repo ──
# These stay on disk (you and the agent still read them during the build); they simply
# never get committed or pushed. The automated scan reads CODE, not these notes — and
# some contain personal emails, the reverse-engineered rubric, and day-of strategy that
# should not be visible to judges or rival participants.
SETUP-BEFORE-EVENT.md
DAY-OF-RUNBOOK.md
SCORING.md
GOOGLE-SERVICES-TRIAL.md
REQUIREMENT-TO-PLAN.md
NOTES.md
README.template.md

# ── AI build config — LOCAL ONLY ──
# Our AGENTS.md is a strategy playbook and .claude/.agents hold skills named
# "promptwars-winning-recipe" etc. No winner published anything like that (the ones who
# kept AI files shipped only a tiny boilerplate AGENTS + one-line CLAUDE). So keep ours
# local: the agent still reads them during the build; the PUBLIC repo stays a clean app
# + product README, matching the FlowMind / carboncut / Pebbl winner format.
AGENTS.md
CLAUDE.md
.claude/
.agents/
